    <div class="prose">
      <p>The last row of Table 1 shows that the realistic setting, where training is temporally precedent to testing, causes the worst classifier performance in the majority of cases.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="footnote">The original paper of DREBIN utilizes a hold-out approach by performing 10 random splits, with 66% of the data used for training and 33% for testing. Since hold-out is nearly equivalent to k-fold cross-validation (CV) and is susceptible to the same spatio-temporal biases, for the sake of simplicity in this section, we refer to a k-fold cross validation setting for both DREBIN and DEEPDREBIN.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="psec" id="method">
    <h2>Space-Time Aware Evaluation</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>To evaluate the classifier, the dataset D must be split into a training dataset Tr with a time window of size W, and a testing dataset Ts with a time window of size S. Here, we consider S &gt; W in order to estimate long-term performance and robustness to decay of the classifier. We emphasize that, although we have the labels of objects in Ts ⊆ D, all the evaluations and tuning algorithms must assume that labels y<sub>i</sub> of objects s<sub>i</sub> ∈ Ts are unknown.</p>
      <p>To evaluate performance over time, the test set Ts must be split into time-slots of size Δ. We now formalize three constraints that must be enforced when dividing D into Tr and Ts for a realistic setting that avoids spatio-temporal experimental bias. While C1 was proposed in past work, we are the first to propose C2 and C3—which we show to be fundamental.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cards" data-augmented-ui="tl-clip br-clip border">
      <div class="card"><h4><span class="cn">C1</span> Temporal training consistency</h4><p>All the objects in the training must be strictly temporally precedent to the testing ones.</p><p class="formula">time(s<sub>i</sub>) &lt; time(s<sub>j</sub>), ∀s<sub>i</sub> ∈ Tr, ∀s<sub>j</sub> ∈ Ts</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h4><span class="cn">C2</span> Temporal gw/mw time-window consistency</h4><p>In every testing slot of size Δ, all test objects s<sub>i</sub> must be from the same time window. If violated, the results are biased because the classifier may learn and test on artifactual behaviors that, for example, distinguish goodware from malware just by their different API versions.</p></div>
      <div class="card"><h4><span class="cn">C3</span> Realistic malware-to-goodware ratio in testing</h4><p>To have a realistic evaluation, the average percentage of malware in the testing (δ) must be as close as possible to the estimated percentage of malware in the wild (σ̂).</p><p class="formula">δ ≃ σ̂</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>If C3 is violated by overestimating the percentage of malware, the results are positively inflated. We highlight that, although the testing distribution δ cannot be changed (in order to get realistic results), the percentage of malware in the training φ may be tuned.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Time-aware Performance Metrics</h3>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>We introduce a time-aware performance metric that allows for the comparison of different classifiers while considering time decay. Point estimates are always to be preferred to represent the real performance of an algorithm. The cumulative estimates can be used to highlight a smoothed trend and to show overall performance up to a certain point, but can be misleading if reported on their own if objects are too sparsely distributed in some test slots Δ.</p>
      <p>To facilitate the comparison of different time decay plots, we define a new metric, <span class="term">Area Under Time</span> (AUT), the area under the performance curve over time.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="callout" data-augmented-ui="tl-clip br-clip">
      <span class="eyebrow">AUT</span>
      <p class="formula">AUT(P, N) = <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>N−1</sub> Σ<sub>k=1</sub><sup>N−1</sup> [P(X<sub>k+1</sub>) + P(X<sub>k</sub>)] / 2</p>
      <p class="callout-note">P(X<sub>k</sub>) is the value of the point estimate of the performance metric P (e.g., F1) evaluated at point X<sub>k</sub> := (W + kΔ); N is the number of test slots, and 1/(N − 1) is a normalization factor so that AUT ∈ [0, 1]. The perfect classifier with robustness to time decay in the time window S has AUT = 1. As an example, AUT(F1, 12m) is the point estimate of F1-Score considering time decay for a period of 12 months, with a 1-month interval.</p>
    </div>
    <blockquote class="excerpt">
      <p>AUT(P,N) is a metric that allows us to evaluate performance P of a malware classifier against time decay over N time units in realistic experimental settings—obtained by enforcing C1, C2, and C3.</p>
    </blockquote>

    <h3>Tuning Training Ratio</h3>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>We propose a novel algorithm that allows for the adjustment of the training ratio φ when the dataset is imbalanced, in order to optimize a user-specified performance metric (F1-Score, Precision, or Recall) on the minority class, subject to a maximum tolerated error, while aiming to reduce time decay. Also, we assume that the user already has a training dataset Tr and wants to use as many objects from it as possible, while still achieving a good performance trade-off; for this purpose, we perform a progressive sub-sampling of the goodware class.</p>
      <p>As a result, if we want to maximize F1-Score for the malware class, we need to limit both FPs and FNs. if P = Pr, we minimize FNs, so we constrain FNR. And if P = Rec, we constrain FPR.</p>
    </div>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li><div><h4>Split the training set</h4><p>Split the training set Tr into two subsets: actual training (ProperTr) and validation set (Val), while enforcing C1, C2, C3, also implying δ = σ̂</p></div></li>
      <li><div><h4>Divide into time-slots</h4><p>Divide Val into N non-overlapped subsets, each corresponding to a time-slot Δ, so that Val<sub>array</sub> = [V<sub>0</sub>, V<sub>1</sub>, ..., V<sub>N</sub>]</p></div></li>
      <li><div><h4>Initialize</h4><p>The maximum performance observed P* and the optimal training ratio φ*<sub>P</sub> are initialized by assuming the estimated in-the-wild malware ratio σ̂ for training; in Android, σ̂ ≈ 10%.</p></div></li>
      <li><div><h4>Grid search</h4><p>The grid-search phase iterates over different values of φ, with a learning rate µ (e.g., µ = 0.05), and keeps as φ*<sub>P</sub> the value leading to the best performance, subject to the error constraint. To reduce the chance of discarding high-quality points while down-sampling goodware, we prioritize the most uncertain points (e.g., points close to the decision boundary of the SVM).</p></div></li>
      <li><div><h4>Keep the best</h4><p>Finally, the grid-search explores multiple values of φ and stores the best ones. To capture time-aware performance, we rely on AUT, and the error rate is computed according to the target P.</p></div></li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="psec" id="framework">
    <h2>TESSERACT Implementation</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>We introduce TESSERACT, an open-source performance evaluation framework that rigorously applies constraints C1, C2, and C3, computes AUT, and possesses the capability to refine a classifier utilizing our proposed tuning algorithm. TESSERACT functions comparably to traditional Python ML libraries, yet it distinctively requires not only the features matrix X and labels y, but also an array of timestamps t, which sorts the dates corresponding to each data entry.</p>
    </div>

