    <h3>Robustness of deep learning approach</h3>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>In our initial analysis documented in the original paper, the deep learning method appeared to outshine the other two traditional ML approaches (Alg1(Drebin) and Alg2(MaMaDroid)) in terms of robustness after enforcing C1, C2 and C3. However, upon closer inspection and a more extended evaluation, this claim does not hold consistently. It is true that during the first two year of evaluation, the deep learning approach demonstrated a better and relatively stable performance. Nonetheless, when considering the broader scope of the analysis, it becomes evident that the overall performance of the deep learning method aligns closely with that of traditional machine learning techniques, if retraining is not possible.</p>
    </div>

    <h3>Tuning algorithm</h3>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>We first aim to maximize P = F1-Score of malware class, subject to E<sub>max</sub> = 10%. After running Algorithm 1 on DREBIN and DEEPDREBIN, we set φ*<sub>F1</sub> = 0.55 for DREBIN, and φ*<sub>F1</sub> = 0.20 for DEEPDREBIN. We remark that the choice of φ*<sub>F1</sub> uses only training information and no test information is used—the optimal value is chosen from a 4-month validation set extracted from the one year of training data; this is to simulate a realistic deployment setting in which we have no a priori information about testing.</p>
      <p>When trained with φ*<sub>F1</sub>, a decrease in Precision is noted due to an increase in False Positives (FPs), while an increase in Recall is observed as a result of a decrease in False Negatives (FNs). This trade-off leads to a slight improvement of AUT for both classifiers. For each specific performance metric P, the tailored performance AUT(P, 48m) with φ*<sub>P</sub> generally surpasses the performance achieved using the original training ratio σ̂ for both DREBIN and DEEPDREBIN. The notable exception is when focusing on Precision; in this case, the tuning slightly underperforms compared to the original training ratio.</p>
    </div>
